                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "35d5af3c-7898-3ac9-ade9-24d309ac3fd9",
                        "entry_id": "32720a60-d03d-3da3-bad6-2f7592c0ef75",
                        "glosses": [
                            "An altered form of scapolite, originally believed to be a unique mineral."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "bc0eb389-a111-4ad3-9ba3-92b672b9df97",
                                "sense_id": "35d5af3c-7898-3ac9-ade9-24d309ac3fd9",
                                "text": "I offer it as a new mineral species which will take a place by the side of edingtonite ; and to connect with his favorite science, the name of one who is among its most successful cultivators, I propose for it the designation of Algerite."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ea8538b2-1a87-4614-871d-c2bfeffd1bee",
                                "sense_id": "35d5af3c-7898-3ac9-ade9-24d309ac3fd9",
                                "text": "Algerite, named by Mr. Hunt in honour of its discoverer, Mr. Alger, is found in the town of Franklin, Sussex County, New Jersey."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0a75ddf6-39cb-4869-b10a-e3875aaa01b6",
                                "sense_id": "35d5af3c-7898-3ac9-ade9-24d309ac3fd9",
                                "text": "By alkaline carbonates in solution, alkalies may be introduced and lime removed, thus forming mica, algerite, etc., and perhaps zeolites; and the potash carbonate appears to be able under some circumstances to replace with potash, the soda of a soda silicate, as in algerite."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ed2abb5d-4b49-4662-b0b5-d60bfbf4c9f7",
                                "sense_id": "35d5af3c-7898-3ac9-ade9-24d309ac3fd9",
                                "text": "Prof. T. Sterry Hunt, of Montreal, has called my attention to the near relation between the composition of the Diana gieseckite and his analyses of the minerals algerite (this Journal, [2], viii, 105) and wilsonite (ib., xix, 428),"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "cf834877-fbc9-4862-bc75-b62f12078e6c",
                                "sense_id": "35d5af3c-7898-3ac9-ade9-24d309ac3fd9",
                                "text": "Kembleite: An obsolete local name, of unknown origin, used in reference to altered scapolite (algerite)."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
